**assistant**:
> This is almost certainly an **aliasing / mutation** issue.
> 
> In Julia, this line:
> 
> ```julia
> regrange = regrangebest
> ```
> 
> does **not** make a copy. It makes `regrange` another name for the exact same array object as `regrangebest`.
> 
> So when you do:
> 
> ```julia
> regrange[i] = ...
> ```
> 
> you are already mutating `regrangebest`.
> 
> That means later:
> 
> ```julia
> if value > valuebest
>     regrangebest = regrange
> end
> ```
> 
> doesn’t visibly “change” anything, because `regrangebest` was already changed before you reached the `if`.
> 
> You probably want:
> 
> ```julia
> regrange = copy(regrangebest)
> ```
> 
> instead of:
> 
> ```julia
> regrange = regrangebest
> ```
> 
> So this block should be:
> 
> ```julia
> println(regrangebest)
> 
> regrange = copy(regrangebest)
> 
> i = mod1(div(ll, 2), length(regrange))
> 
> regrange[i] =
>     rand.(Truncated.(Normal.(min.(dfbest.regrange_maxkw, regrangebest), exp(-ll / 100)),
>                      0., dfbest.regrange_maxkw))[i]
> 
> println(regrange)
> ```
> 
> I also changed this:
> 
> ```julia
> div(ll, 2) % length(regrange)
> ```
> 
> to:
> 
> ```julia
> mod1(div(ll, 2), length(regrange))
> ```
> 
> because Julia arrays are 1-indexed. The expression `x % n` can return `0`, which is not a valid normal array index in Julia. `mod1(x, n)` cycles through `1:n`.
> 
> The core bug is here:
> 
> ```julia
> regrange = regrangebest
> ```
> 
> Use `copy` whenever you want to try a candidate perturbation without modifying the incumbent best solution. Also, when accepting the improvement, I’d write:
> 
> ```julia
> regrangebest = copy(regrange)
> ```
> 
> to avoid future accidental aliasing. Likewise, if `strat` is mutable, you may need to copy that too.
